---
import DocShell from '../../layouts/doc-shell.astro';
import { docsLinks } from '../../lib/nav';

const toc = [
   { id: 'support', label: 'Supported screen readers' },
   { id: 'record', label: 'Record a session' },
   { id: 'permissions', label: 'macOS Screen Recording permission' },
   { id: 'troubleshoot', label: 'Troubleshooting' },
   { id: 'privacy', label: 'Privacy' },
];
---

<DocShell
   title="Record a session"
   summary="Capture a VoiceOver session to a .mov file or an NVDA session to an .mp4 file. Attach the video with the transcript when a bug report needs to show what happened on screen as well as what the screen reader announced."
   links={docsLinks}
   toc={toc}
>
   <section class="panel" id="support">
      <h2>Supported screen readers</h2>
      <div class="route-list">
         <div class="route-row">
            <code>voiceover</code>
            <span>macOS. Records to a <code>.mov</code> path through native video capture.</span>
         </div>
         <div class="route-row">
            <code>nvda</code>
            <span>Windows. Records to an <code>.mp4</code> path.</span>
         </div>
         <div class="route-row">
            <code>virtual</code>
            <span>Not supported. The simulated screen reader has no window to capture. It produces a transcript instead.</span>
         </div>
      </div>
      <p>
         On macOS a11ied captures video only. It does not request microphone access, so the file
         has no audio track.
      </p>
   </section>

   <section class="panel" id="record">
      <h2>Record a session</h2>
      <p>
         Pass <code>--recording</code> with an output path when you start the session. The file
         closes when you stop the session.
      </p>
      <pre><code>a1 sr start --sr voiceover --recording ./recordings/checkout.mov http://localhost:3000/checkout --json
a1 sr next --json
a1 sr checkpoint "submitted the form" --json
a1 sr transcript --json
a1 sr stop --json</code></pre>
      <p>
         The recording covers the whole session, so keep each session short and limited to one
         behavior. Add <code>sr checkpoint</code> calls to mark the moments you want a reviewer to
         look for.
      </p>
      <p>
         Stopping writes the Markdown transcript next to the recording. For
         <code>./recordings/checkout.mov</code> that file is
         <code>./recordings/checkout.transcript.md</code>. It has an ISO timestamp per
         announcement, so a reviewer can match what the screen reader said to the point in the
         video.
      </p>
   </section>

   <section class="panel" id="permissions">
      <h2>macOS Screen Recording permission</h2>
      <p>
         macOS grants screen capture to the application that runs the command, not to a11ied. If you
         run <code>a1</code> from Terminal, Terminal needs the permission. If your editor or agent
         runs it, that app needs it.
      </p>
      <pre><code>a1 doctor</code></pre>
      <p>
         On macOS, <code>doctor</code> records a few seconds of screen video with
         <code>screencapture</code> for the app that ran it, to confirm the permission works, and
         reports the result under the VoiceOver checks. Run it from the same app you plan to
         test from.
      </p>
   </section>

   <section class="panel" id="troubleshoot">
      <h2>Troubleshooting</h2>
      <div class="route-list">
         <div class="route-row">
            <strong>Works in Terminal, fails in your editor</strong>
            <span>Grant Screen Recording to the editor in System Settings, then restart it.</span>
         </div>
         <div class="route-row">
            <strong>The command fails before recording starts</strong>
            <span>Install Chrome, Edge, Brave, or Chromium. Opening the page for VoiceOver or NVDA
            uses the system browser, not Playwright, so one needs to be installed.</span>
         </div>
         <div class="route-row">
            <strong>doctor reports requires-setup for VoiceOver or NVDA</strong>
            <span>Run <code>a1 setup</code>, which runs the Guidepup steps <code>doctor</code> lists under Action items and re-checks afterward.</span>
         </div>
         <div class="route-row">
            <strong>The file is empty</strong>
            <span>Stop the session with <code>sr stop</code>. The recorder finishes the file when the session stops.</span>
         </div>
      </div>
   </section>

   <section class="field-note" id="privacy">
      <h2>Privacy</h2>
      <p>
         A recording captures the screen, which includes the page content, the browser window, system
         menus, and anything else visible during the run. Use test data, not real accounts, and check
         the file before attaching it to a ticket.
      </p>
      <p>
         A recording documents one run against one build. Record the route, the browser, the
         screen reader, and the file path with it, so a reviewer can tell later which build it
         shows.
      </p>
   </section>
</DocShell>
